{{!
    @template theme_dimensions/category_areas

    The areas of a page: one poster per area and, between the rows, the panel of
    the open one. The same partial draws the root's directory and the first
    section of a category page organised by area, so a poster looks and behaves
    the same on both. category_listing::areas() builds the context and holds the
    rule behind each flag.

    The posters are a grid of list items. Past the slice the server cuts at, the
    rest sit inside a native disclosure below the grid, with a grid of their own;
    it is drawn open when the open poster is among them, or the panel would be
    drawn where nobody can see it.

    The panel is one list item per page, always in the document: inside the grid
    that holds the open poster, right after that poster's item, or, when nothing
    is open, empty and hidden at the end of the main grid, where the client
    module fills it. Its style attribute carries the five integers that place its
    row and its arrow per breakpoint, and the open area's band, which paints its
    rail, its head and its glow; integers and one hex colour printed back by
    branding::hex() are that attribute's whole vocabulary, so this CSS-context
    sink can only ever hold numbers and a colour.

    Without JavaScript every poster is a link: an expandable one opens its panel
    (landing on it by the fragment), the open one closes it (landing back on the
    poster), and one with nothing to open is the area's own page. The hint under
    the grid goes once a panel is open. The status paragraph sits outside both
    grids and is never re-created, so a screen reader keeps tracking it while the
    client rewrites its text after every load.

    On a category page the link to the courses sitting directly in the category
    is written beside the heading, hidden while that section is, so the client
    can offer it again; the root has no such section.

    Under a search or a filter the section says so (data-filtered) and carries
    the sentence a zero panel's announcement ends with (data-elsewhere). Every
    poster wears its number as a pill; a poster with no result leaves its grid
    through the hidden attribute on its item - never removed, and never the open
    one - and the areas with no result are named in a closed disclosure after the
    grids, each a link to the area's own page without the criterion (or its name
    as text where the reader has no page to open), the open one's entry hidden
    while it stays in the grid. That disclosure carries both of its sentences, so
    the client swaps them without composing anything. The reveal past the slice
    opens by itself when it holds a result, and is hidden when it holds neither a
    result nor the open poster. When nothing matched anywhere and no panel is
    open, a box says so, with the page's way out of every filter and, on a
    category page searched for a term, a link to the root carrying the term,
    offered only where the reader may open the root. Each of these is in the
    document on every page, hidden when it has nothing to say, because the client
    applies filters in place and shows them again. The root reads its search
    alone, so its box speaks of the search and its way out clears it.

    The example draws both states at once - a poster at rest beside one wearing
    its pill and a zero poster the criterion hid, with the summary and the
    "nothing matches" box - so that every region is rendered and linted from one
    context. A real page is at rest or narrowed, never both.

